1
00:00:00,718 --> 00:00:00,910
How

2
00:00:01,521 --> 00:00:04,960
do we make sure that our agent knows

3
00:00:05,555 --> 00:00:07,563
about its general instructions, but most

4
00:00:07,563 --> 00:00:09,945
importantly also about the tools

5
00:00:10,559 --> 00:00:11,077
it may call?

6
00:00:12,872 --> 00:00:15,362
So how do we make our agent, our brain, our

7
00:00:15,476 --> 00:00:17,416
AI brain, which makes the decisions in the

8
00:00:17,416 --> 00:00:19,436
end, aware of the tools it may use?

9
00:00:20,065 --> 00:00:22,273
In a primitive agent, as we're building it

10
00:00:22,273 --> 00:00:24,159
here, we can do that via the instructions.

11
00:00:25,135 --> 00:00:27,687
The instructions do contain general

12
00:00:27,736 --> 00:00:28,354
instructions

13
00:00:28,874 --> 00:00:29,947
that describe the task,

14
00:00:30,780 --> 00:00:33,185
the agent should perform, that is important,

15
00:00:33,699 --> 00:00:36,425
but they also need to contain information

16
00:00:36,425 --> 00:00:39,216
about the tools that are available. You

17
00:00:39,216 --> 00:00:41,959
learned that earlier in the course. An agent

18
00:00:41,959 --> 00:00:45,535
receives a lot of invisible inputs besides

19
00:00:45,568 --> 00:00:48,182
the user input. The user input is part of

20
00:00:48,214 --> 00:00:50,026
what we send to the agent, but not

21
00:00:50,283 --> 00:00:50,668
everything.

22
00:00:51,326 --> 00:00:53,748
An agent, or the AI model that powers the

23
00:00:53,748 --> 00:00:56,169
agent, I should say, does not just receive

24
00:00:56,169 --> 00:00:58,639
the user input, but also some general

25
00:00:58,639 --> 00:01:00,452
instructions, And very important,

26
00:01:00,805 --> 00:01:03,035
descriptions about the available tools. And

27
00:01:03,115 --> 00:01:04,896
that is what's part of our instructions here

28
00:01:04,896 --> 00:01:05,073
too.

29
00:01:05,923 --> 00:01:08,025
I know that the agent we're building here,

30
00:01:08,265 --> 00:01:10,480
because I'm the developer of the agent, has

31
00:01:10,816 --> 00:01:14,330
2 main tools, summarize text and create PDF.

32
00:01:14,827 --> 00:01:16,817
And if you are building your own agent, you

33
00:01:16,849 --> 00:01:17,844
put your tools here.

34
00:01:18,389 --> 00:01:20,218
So this is crucial. This is extremely

35
00:01:20,218 --> 00:01:22,833
important. It describes to the agent, to the

36
00:01:22,930 --> 00:01:25,850
AI model, which tool calls it may request.

37
00:01:26,395 --> 00:01:28,320
This is how we pass that information to the

38
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:29,795
model. In a primitive way.

39
00:01:30,485 --> 00:01:32,793
Well, then I also have this important part

40
00:01:32,793 --> 00:01:35,117
here. This describes to the model which kind

41
00:01:35,117 --> 00:01:36,608
of reply I wanna get back,

42
00:01:37,442 --> 00:01:40,423
that I want a JSON-formatted reply.

43
00:01:41,289 --> 00:01:43,726
JSON is just text, as you can tell, but it's

44
00:01:43,870 --> 00:01:45,248
in a certain format that's more

45
00:01:45,313 --> 00:01:46,098
machine-readable.

46
00:01:46,835 --> 00:01:49,048
And I want either this response, which

47
00:01:49,096 --> 00:01:50,170
describes a tool call.

48
00:01:50,763 --> 00:01:52,622
So I make it very clear that if the

49
00:01:53,151 --> 00:01:55,893
model wants to use a tool, a function, it

50
00:01:55,893 --> 00:01:58,409
should reply like this, giving me the name

51
00:01:58,457 --> 00:02:00,557
of the tool it wants to call and the input.

52
00:02:00,702 --> 00:02:02,949
It wants to pass to that tool. So the text

53
00:02:02,949 --> 00:02:04,490
that should be summarized, for example.

54
00:02:05,421 --> 00:02:07,411
And if it decides that it does not want to

55
00:02:07,427 --> 00:02:09,691
call a tool, it should reply with this

56
00:02:09,691 --> 00:02:10,044
format.

57
00:02:10,959 --> 00:02:13,238
Now, why such a strange looking machine

58
00:02:13,302 --> 00:02:15,678
readable format? Because I need to analyze

59
00:02:15,710 --> 00:02:18,262
the response that I get back from the model

60
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:21,393
with code. Because in my agent loop, we get

61
00:02:21,393 --> 00:02:23,656
back a response from our brain. And then in

62
00:02:23,656 --> 00:02:24,234
the next step,

63
00:02:24,844 --> 00:02:27,685
I use some Python logic for parsing that

64
00:02:27,813 --> 00:02:30,205
JSON data, which I get back. So this clearly

65
00:02:30,221 --> 00:02:31,425
formatted response.

66
00:02:31,987 --> 00:02:34,858
And I check if we got a final answer in

67
00:02:34,890 --> 00:02:35,067
there.

68
00:02:35,692 --> 00:02:37,746
Remember in my instructions, I told it to

69
00:02:37,762 --> 00:02:40,040
put final answer in there if it has that

70
00:02:40,040 --> 00:02:40,730
final answer.

71
00:02:41,452 --> 00:02:43,184
And I check therefore if I have that. And if

72
00:02:43,233 --> 00:02:43,586
I do,

73
00:02:44,259 --> 00:02:47,773
I return that final answer here, which will

74
00:02:47,885 --> 00:02:50,452
break out of that loop. Returning in a

75
00:02:50,452 --> 00:02:52,906
function in all programming languages

76
00:02:52,955 --> 00:02:56,388
essentially breaks out of any loop that code

77
00:02:56,388 --> 00:02:58,345
may have been running in. So that puts us

78
00:02:58,537 --> 00:03:00,527
out of that agent loop because we're done

79
00:03:00,719 --> 00:03:01,377
with the task.

80
00:03:02,244 --> 00:03:04,154
But if we make it past this if check, we

81
00:03:04,234 --> 00:03:06,706
don't have a final answer yet. In that case,

82
00:03:06,738 --> 00:03:09,129
I analyze which tool call it requests

83
00:03:09,306 --> 00:03:10,991
because that's the only other option, right?

84
00:03:11,377 --> 00:03:13,592
We either got a final answer or a tool call

85
00:03:13,640 --> 00:03:16,192
request. There are no other options in this

86
00:03:16,192 --> 00:03:18,760
agentic loop and pretty much in all agentic

87
00:03:18,760 --> 00:03:18,985
loops.

88
00:03:19,803 --> 00:03:21,858
You either want to do something that may

89
00:03:22,050 --> 00:03:24,426
include activating some skill in more

90
00:03:24,538 --> 00:03:26,400
complex agents, but you want to do something

91
00:03:26,721 --> 00:03:28,679
or you get a final answer. So in this case

92
00:03:28,792 --> 00:03:30,316
here, if we don't have a final answer, we

93
00:03:30,332 --> 00:03:32,547
want to call a tool. I find out which tool

94
00:03:32,580 --> 00:03:32,949
that is.

95
00:03:33,703 --> 00:03:35,580
I then check my max rounds. That is what I

96
00:03:35,869 --> 00:03:38,003
mentioned earlier. I keep track of how many

97
00:03:38,019 --> 00:03:40,281
rounds we already had. I increment this

98
00:03:40,281 --> 00:03:42,993
counter here at the end of each round. And I

99
00:03:42,993 --> 00:03:45,062
make sure that if we called too many tools,

100
00:03:45,720 --> 00:03:45,913
we

101
00:03:46,523 --> 00:03:49,330
throw an error in the end because the agent

102
00:03:49,330 --> 00:03:51,352
seemingly got stuck. And as mentioned

103
00:03:51,416 --> 00:03:53,261
earlier, you want to increase the max tool

104
00:03:53,341 --> 00:03:55,684
rounds if that breaks your agent. It's just

105
00:03:55,684 --> 00:03:58,347
a security mechanism to avoid loops that

106
00:03:58,476 --> 00:04:00,096
take too long because of course you're

107
00:04:00,096 --> 00:04:01,797
paying for all these requests.

108
00:04:02,584 --> 00:04:04,672
Now, if you make it past this check, we take

109
00:04:04,672 --> 00:04:06,840
a look at the arguments the model wants to

110
00:04:06,840 --> 00:04:08,928
pass to the tool. So the input it wants to

111
00:04:08,928 --> 00:04:09,618
pass to the tool.

112
00:04:10,518 --> 00:04:12,461
I also then check if the tool name it

113
00:04:12,461 --> 00:04:14,822
requested is in my registered tool

114
00:04:14,870 --> 00:04:15,480
functions.

115
00:04:16,219 --> 00:04:17,552
That is this part here.

116
00:04:18,082 --> 00:04:20,025
I check simply if it's one of the tools I

117
00:04:20,025 --> 00:04:23,061
support. And of course, the tools I describe

118
00:04:23,061 --> 00:04:25,823
here must match the things I look for

119
00:04:26,273 --> 00:04:28,617
because that is what the model will request.

120
00:04:28,682 --> 00:04:30,577
So I must check if that is what I wanna use

121
00:04:30,609 --> 00:04:31,171
in my code.

122
00:04:31,813 --> 00:04:34,590
And I throw an error if an invalid tool was

123
00:04:34,622 --> 00:04:37,334
requested, the same if the arguments don't

124
00:04:37,350 --> 00:04:37,831
match up.

125
00:04:38,618 --> 00:04:40,431
But if you make it past all these checks, I

126
00:04:40,560 --> 00:04:43,208
have this code here which actually executes

127
00:04:43,208 --> 00:04:44,973
the tool, which executes the function.

128
00:04:46,177 --> 00:04:48,087
And that's also what you learned earlier in

129
00:04:48,087 --> 00:04:51,377
the course. Tools run in the agent harness

130
00:04:51,457 --> 00:04:54,041
on the system that runs the agent loop. And

131
00:04:54,041 --> 00:04:54,876
that's the same here.

132
00:04:55,405 --> 00:04:58,984
The tool call, the function, is executed on

133
00:04:59,016 --> 00:05:01,534
my system, on your system if you're running

134
00:05:01,582 --> 00:05:04,983
this code, not in the cloud, not on the AI

135
00:05:05,032 --> 00:05:08,080
server, instead in the harness. And this

136
00:05:08,080 --> 00:05:10,534
entire thing here is the harness that runs

137
00:05:10,566 --> 00:05:11,208
the agent loop.

138
00:05:12,395 --> 00:05:14,416
Then we got back a result from the tool

139
00:05:14,416 --> 00:05:14,625
call,

140
00:05:15,283 --> 00:05:17,593
and I then put that into my conversation, in

141
00:05:17,625 --> 00:05:19,694
my history, my chat history, so to say.

142
00:05:20,336 --> 00:05:23,721
I store the tool result for the tool name so

143
00:05:23,801 --> 00:05:26,127
that the next time we send that history to

144
00:05:26,127 --> 00:05:28,888
the model, it is aware of that the tool call

145
00:05:29,017 --> 00:05:30,992
was made and what the result was.

146
00:05:31,828 --> 00:05:33,354
And then we're still in the loop, so we

147
00:05:33,354 --> 00:05:36,727
continue until we got a final answer or we

148
00:05:36,727 --> 00:05:38,076
run out of rounds in the end.

149
00:05:38,638 --> 00:05:40,662
That is how the entire agentic loop here

150
00:05:40,742 --> 00:05:44,100
works. And that's how any AI agent works in

151
00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:46,509
a nutshell. It may have more complex

152
00:05:46,509 --> 00:05:48,661
handling logic, it may have more complex

153
00:05:48,758 --> 00:05:51,280
tools, but it's always that logic, sending

154
00:05:51,328 --> 00:05:53,721
requests, handling the response, calling

155
00:05:53,753 --> 00:05:55,392
tools if necessary, and then

156
00:05:56,067 --> 00:05:58,075
also handling the fact that at some point it

157
00:05:58,075 --> 00:05:58,541
should be done.

158
00:05:59,537 --> 00:06:01,336
And that is really all that's going on here.
